QB.add(3,[{"id":3001,"t":"Body Regions","s":"The image shows a person in the anatomical position. Name the region labelled <b>A</b>.","e":"A marks the axilla, the region at the junction of the upper limb and the thorax, just below the shoulder. Using the correct region gives context to terms and planes, e.g. “a swelling in the right axilla”.","k":"A = axilla.","img":{"src":"3/regions","cap":"Labels removed."},"a":["axilla","armpit","axillary region"],"rn":[["","Which 3 upper parts make up the upper limb?","Which 3 upper parts make up the upper limb?","Shoulder and axilla; Elbow; Forearm"]]},{"id":3002,"t":"Body Regions","s":"The image shows a person in the anatomical position. Name the region of the upper limb labelled <b>B</b> (between the elbow and the wrist).","e":"B lies between the elbow and the wrist: the forearm. Note “arm” in anatomy means only the part between shoulder and elbow, so the forearm is a separate region of the free part of the upper limb.","k":"B = forearm.","img":{"src":"3/regions","cap":"Labels removed."},"a":["forearm"],"rn":[["","Which 3 upper parts make up the upper limb?","Which 3 upper parts make up the upper limb?","Shoulder and axilla; Elbow; Forearm"],["","In pronation, which part rotates?","In pronation, which part rotates?","The forearm; Not the arm above elbow"]]},{"id":3003,"t":"What Is Anatomy","s":"A pathologist examines the shape of a liver with the naked eye, then a stained slice under a microscope. Which branches of anatomy are these, in order?","o":["Gross anatomy, then histology","Microscopic anatomy, then gross anatomy","Surface anatomy, then gross anatomy","Gross anatomy, then regional anatomy","Histology, then living anatomy"],"e":"<b>Gross anatomy</b> covers structures visible <b>without magnification</b>; <b>microscopic anatomy (histology)</b> covers structures visible <b>only with magnification</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Not the two branches named on the slide.","Regional is a way of teaching, not the microscopic branch.","Reversed, and living anatomy is a practical session type."],"k":"Gross = no magnification; microscopic (histology) = with magnification.","rn":[["","Gross vs microscopic anatomy","Gross vs microscopic anatomy","Gross: seen without magnification; Microscopic: needs magnification"],["","Microscopic anatomy is also called?","Microscopic anatomy is also called?","Histology"]]},{"id":3004,"t":"What Is Anatomy","s":"When the word “anatomy” is used on its own, which branch does it usually mean?","e":"Typically, when used alone, “anatomy” means gross anatomy – structures visible without magnification.","k":"Anatomy alone = gross anatomy.","a":["gross anatomy","gross"],"rn":[["","Used alone, what does \"anatomy\" mean?","Used alone, what does \"anatomy\" mean?","Gross anatomy"]]},{"id":3005,"t":"Body Regions","s":"Name the region labelled <b>C</b>, described as a superficial part of the pelvis.","e":"C marks the perineum, a superficial part of the pelvis, between the thighs below the abdomen.","k":"C = perineum.","img":{"src":"3/regions","cap":"Labels removed."},"a":["perineum","perineal region"],"rn":[["","What is the perineum?","What is the perineum?","Superficial part of the pelvis"]]},{"id":3006,"t":"Body Regions","s":"Name the region of the lower limb labelled <b>D</b>.","e":"D lies between the hip and the knee: the thigh. The free part of the lower limb is thigh, knee, leg, ankle and foot.","k":"D = thigh.","img":{"src":"3/regions","cap":"Labels removed."},"a":["thigh"],"rn":[["","Which 3 upper parts make up the lower limb?","Which 3 upper parts make up the lower limb?","Hip; Thigh; Knee"]]},{"id":3007,"t":"Body Regions","s":"A clinician writes “laceration on the anterior surface of the region labelled <b>E</b>”. Name region E using the correct anatomical term.","e":"E lies between the knee and the ankle. In anatomy this region is the “leg” (the whole limb is the lower limb; the part above the knee is the thigh).","k":"E = leg (knee to ankle).","img":{"src":"3/regions","cap":"Labels removed."},"a":["leg","lower leg"],"rn":[["","Which part of the lower limb lies between the knee and ankle?","Which part of the lower limb lies between the knee and ankle?","Leg"]]},{"id":3008,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"In the image, the dark blue bar crosses the subject’s lower limb horizontally, and the red bar runs vertically down the trunk. Which description of the <b>blue</b> bar is correct?","o":["Transverse plane through the left leg, just proximal to the ankle","Transverse plane through the right leg, just proximal to the ankle, as seen by the viewer","Coronal plane through the left leg, just distal to the knee","Transverse plane through the left leg, just distal to the ankle","Sagittal plane through the left leg, just proximal to the ankle"],"e":"A horizontal plane separating superior from inferior is transverse (axial/horizontal). It crosses the subject’s left leg (on the viewer’s right, since the subject faces you) just above the ankle – proximal, i.e. closer to the trunk.","w":["Sides are always the subject’s own: the bar is on the subject’s left leg, which appears on the viewer’s right.","A coronal plane is vertical, separating anterior and posterior.","The bar lies above the ankle, nearer the trunk – proximal.","A sagittal plane is vertical, separating right and left."],"k":"Use the subject’s left/right; transverse = horizontal.","img":{"src":"3/regions","cap":"Labels removed."},"rn":[["","Which plane would pass through the left leg just proximal to the ankle?","Which plane would pass through the left leg just proximal to the ankle?","Transverse plane"],["","What is an example of combining regions, planes and terms?","What is an example of combining regions, planes and terms?","Transverse plane through left leg; Just proximal to the ankle"]]},{"id":3009,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"The red vertical bar in the image runs down the front of the trunk between the midline and the nipple. Which description is correct?","o":["Sagittal plane through the thorax, medial to the nipple","Coronal plane through the thorax, medial to the nipple","Sagittal plane through the thorax, lateral to the nipple","Median plane through the thorax, in the midline","Transverse plane through the thorax, at the nipple"],"e":"A vertical plane separating right from left is sagittal; one not in the midline is not the median plane. The bar lies between the midline and the nipple, so it is medial to the nipple.","w":["A coronal plane would separate front from back, not run front to back.","The bar lies nearer the midline than the nipple – medial.","It is off the midline, so it is not the median plane.","The bar is vertical, not horizontal."],"k":"Vertical right/left = sagittal; off-midline ≠ median.","img":{"src":"3/regions","cap":"Labels removed."},"rn":[["","Which plane would pass through the thorax medial to the nipple?","Which plane would pass through the thorax medial to the nipple?","Sagittal plane"]]},{"id":3010,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"A CT scanner produces images as if the patient were cut into horizontal slices, each viewed from below. A radiologist wants an image that shows the heart and both lungs side by side with the vertebral column at the bottom of the image. Which plane is needed, and which plane would instead show the left and right lungs AND the liver below the diaphragm in one image?","o":["Transverse; coronal","Coronal; transverse","Sagittal; coronal","Transverse; median","Axial; sagittal"],"e":"A horizontal (transverse/axial) slice shows structures at one level with anterior at the top and posterior (vertebral column) at the bottom. To show superior–inferior AND right–left relations together (both lungs above, liver below), a vertical plane separating anterior from posterior – coronal – is needed.","w":["Swapped: a single horizontal slice cannot show structures above and below the diaphragm together.","A sagittal plane separates right from left, so it cannot show both lungs side by side.","The median plane shows only midline structures, not both lungs and the liver.","A sagittal plane would show one lung only."],"k":"Transverse = one level; coronal = up–down and left–right.","rn":[["","What are the 4 anatomical planes?","What are the 4 anatomical planes?","Coronal; Sagittal; Midsagittal; Transverse"],["","Coronal plane","Coronal plane","Vertical; Separates anterior and posterior"]]},{"id":3011,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"A wound is on the front of the right forearm, halfway between the elbow and the wrist, on the thumb side. With the patient in the anatomical position, which description is correct?","o":["Anterior, lateral forearm, distal to the elbow","Anterior, medial forearm, proximal to the elbow","Posterior, lateral forearm, distal to the elbow","Anterior, medial forearm, distal to the wrist","Anterior, lateral forearm, proximal to the elbow and distal to the wrist"],"e":"In the anatomical position the palms face forwards and thumbs point out to the side, so the thumb side of the forearm is lateral and the front is anterior. Halfway down the forearm the wound is distal to the elbow (further from the trunk) and proximal to the wrist.","w":["In the anatomical position the thumb is lateral (thumbs out to the side), and the wound is below the elbow.","The palm faces forwards, so the front of the forearm is anterior.","The thumb side is lateral and the wound is proximal to the wrist.","Being proximal to the elbow and distal to the wrist at once is impossible on the forearm."],"k":"Anatomical position: thumb = lateral, palm = anterior.","rn":[["","The wrist is Distal to the elbow","The wrist is Distal to the elbow",""],["","What is an example of distal?","What is an example of distal?","Wrist is distal to elbow"]]},{"id":3012,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"A patient has weakness of the left leg after a lesion on the left side of the spinal cord, and loss of pain sensation in the right leg. Relative to the lesion, how are these two findings described?","o":["Weakness ipsilateral; sensory loss contralateral","Weakness contralateral; sensory loss ipsilateral","Both ipsilateral","Both contralateral","Weakness medial; sensory loss lateral"],"e":"Ipsilateral = on the same side; contralateral = on the opposite side. The left-sided weakness is on the same side as the left-sided lesion (ipsilateral); the right-sided sensory loss is on the opposite side (contralateral).","w":["Swapped: the weakness is on the same (left) side.","The sensory loss is on the opposite side.","The weakness is on the same side.","Medial/lateral describe position relative to the midline, not sides."],"k":"Same side = ipsilateral; opposite = contralateral.","rn":[["","Which pairs of terms describe side?","Which pairs of terms describe side?","Right or left; Ipsilateral or contralateral"]]},{"id":3013,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"A surgeon removes a tumour from beneath a muscle in the anterior thigh. The skin incision lies over the tumour. Which statement correctly relates the skin, the muscle and the tumour?","o":["Skin is superficial to muscle, which is superficial to the tumour","Skin is deep to muscle, which is deep to the tumour","The tumour is superficial to the muscle, and skin is deepest","Muscle is superficial to skin and to the tumour","Skin and tumour are both superficial to the muscle"],"e":"Superficial = closer to the surface; deep = further away from the surface. Skin is most superficial, the muscle is deep to the skin, and the tumour beneath the muscle is the deepest of the three.","w":["Reversed: deep means further from the surface.","The tumour lies beneath the muscle, so it is deeper.","Skin is the most superficial layer.","The tumour is beneath the muscle."],"k":"Superficial → deep: skin, muscle, tumour."},{"id":3014,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"In a description of the head, a structure is said to lie “rostral” to another. In a description of the trunk of an upright adult, which pair of terms means the same as superior and inferior?","o":["Cranial and caudal","Caudal and cranial","Anterior and posterior","Proximal and distal","Ventral and dorsal"],"e":"Cranial (towards the head) and caudal (towards the tail) can replace superior and inferior on the trunk. Rostral is used in embryology and in the head, relative to the nose.","w":["Reversed: cranial is towards the head (superior).","These describe front and back.","Proximal/distal relate to the trunk or origin, mainly in limbs.","Ventral = anterior, dorsal = posterior."],"k":"Cranial = superior; caudal = inferior.","rn":[["","Which terms may replace superior and inferior?","Which terms may replace superior and inferior?","Cranial and caudal"]]},{"id":3015,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"On the posterior trunk, a lesion lies 4 cm from the midline at the level of the lowest ribs. A second lesion lies 10 cm from the midline at the level of the shoulder blade. How is the first lesion best described relative to the second?","o":["Medial and inferior","Lateral and superior","Medial and superior","Lateral and inferior","Proximal and deep"],"e":"Medial = towards the midline, lateral = away from it; superior = towards the head, inferior = towards the feet. The first lesion is closer to the midline and lower: medial and inferior.","w":["It is nearer the midline (medial) and lower (inferior).","It is lower, so inferior, not superior.","It is nearer the midline, so medial.","Proximal/distal are not used for trunk positions like this, and depth is not given."],"k":"Combine axes: medial/lateral + superior/inferior.","rn":[["","Medial","Medial","towards the midline"]]},{"id":3016,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"A fracture of the humerus is near the elbow, and a second fracture of the same bone is near the shoulder. Which description of the fracture near the elbow is correct?","o":["Distal","Proximal","Medial","Cranial","Superficial"],"e":"Proximal = closer to the trunk or origin; distal = further from it. On the humerus (arm), the elbow end is further from the trunk, so that fracture is distal.","w":["The elbow end is further from the trunk.","Medial refers to the midline.","Cranial means towards the head.","Depth is not the comparison here."],"k":"Limbs: proximal near trunk, distal away.","rn":[["","Distal","Distal","Further from trunk or origin"]]},{"id":3017,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"Which pair of anatomical terms are <b>synonyms</b> rather than opposites?","o":["Posterior and dorsal","Proximal and distal","Superficial and deep","Medial and lateral","Ipsilateral and contralateral"],"e":"Anterior = ventral and posterior = dorsal are synonyms. Superficial/deep, medial/lateral, proximal/distal and ipsilateral/contralateral are opposite pairs.","w":["Opposites.","Opposites.","Opposites.","Opposites."],"k":"Posterior = dorsal; anterior = ventral.","rn":[["","Posterior (dorsal)","Posterior (dorsal)","towards the back"]]},{"id":3018,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A patient needs a procedure on the posterior surface of the trunk, and afterwards an examination of the abdomen. Which sequence of named positions is used?","o":["Prone, then supine","Supine, then prone","Fowler’s, then Trendelenburg","Left lateral recumbent, then prone","Trendelenburg, then supine"],"e":"Prone = lying face down (e.g. spinal surgery, access to the back). Supine = lying on the back (e.g. examination of the abdomen).","w":["Reversed: prone is face down, giving access to the back.","Fowler’s and Trendelenburg are for head/neck/shoulder surgery and fainting or gynaecological surgery.","Abdominal examination uses supine, not prone.","Trendelenburg is for fainting or gynaecological surgery."],"k":"Prone face down; supine face up.","rn":[["","What are the 2 lying positions on the front or back?","What are the 2 lying positions on the front or back?","Prone; Supine"]]},{"id":3019,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A patient is lying on their back on a tilting table that is lowered at the head end after collapsing, so the head is lower than the feet. Which named position is this?","o":["Trendelenburg","Fowler’s","Supine","Prone","Right lateral recumbent"],"e":"Trendelenburg is lying on the back with the head end lowered; it is listed for patients who have fainted and, with the lower limbs in lithotomy, for gynaecological surgery.","w":["Fowler’s is sitting/semi-sitting, used for head, neck and shoulder surgery.","Plain supine is flat, without head-down tilt.","Prone is face down.","Lateral recumbent is lying on one side."],"k":"Head down tilt = Trendelenburg.","rn":[["","What is the Trendelenburg position used for?","What is the Trendelenburg position used for?","Patients who have fainted; Lower limb surgery; Gynaecological surgery (lithotomy)"]]},{"id":3020,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"Which pairing of procedure and position is correct?","o":["Surgery on the right kidney – left lateral recumbent","Surgery on the right kidney – right lateral recumbent","Digital rectal examination – right lateral recumbent, knees extended","Shoulder surgery – Trendelenburg","Spinal surgery – supine"],"e":"For kidney or hip surgery the patient lies on the opposite side so the operated side faces up: right kidney → left lateral recumbent. Rectal examination: left lateral, knees flexed. Shoulder, head and neck surgery: Fowler’s. Spinal surgery: prone.","w":["Lying on the right side would put the right kidney against the table.","Rectal examination uses left lateral recumbent with knees flexed.","Shoulder surgery uses Fowler’s.","Spinal surgery uses prone."],"k":"Lie on the opposite side to the operated organ.","rn":[["","What is the right lateral recumbent position used for?","What is the right lateral recumbent position used for?","Left hip surgery; Left renal surgery"],["","What surgery uses the left lateral recumbent position?","What surgery uses the left lateral recumbent position?","Right hip surgery; Right renal surgery"],["","What are the 2 lying positions on the side?","What are the 2 lying positions on the side?","Right lateral recumbent; Left lateral recumbent"]]},{"id":3021,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"A parasagittal plane passes 3 cm to the left of the midline through the head. Which statement about this plane is correct?","o":["It separates unequal right and left parts","It separates equal right and left halves","It separates anterior and posterior parts","It is the same as the median plane","It separates superior and inferior parts"],"e":"Any sagittal plane is vertical and separates right from left. Only the midsagittal (median) plane lies in the midline and gives equal halves; a plane 3 cm to the side gives unequal parts.","w":["Only the median (midsagittal) plane gives equal halves.","That is a coronal plane.","The median plane is in the midline.","That is a transverse plane."],"k":"Sagittal off midline = unequal right/left.","rn":[["","Sagittal plane","Sagittal plane","Vertical; Separates right and left"]]},{"id":3022,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A patient lies supine with the knee straight and lifts the whole lower limb towards the ceiling. Which movement is occurring at the hip, and how does the angle at the hip change?","o":["Flexion; the angle decreases","Extension; the angle increases","Flexion; the angle increases","Abduction; the angle decreases","Extension; the angle decreases"],"e":"Flexion decreases the angle at a joint and extension increases it. Lifting the straight limb forwards brings the thigh towards the front of the trunk, closing the angle at the hip: flexion.","w":["Moving the thigh forwards towards the trunk is flexion, which decreases the angle.","Flexion decreases the angle.","Abduction moves the limb away from the midline, sideways.","Extension increases the angle."],"k":"Flexion closes the joint angle.","rn":[["","Flexion","Flexion","Decreases the joint angle"]]},{"id":3023,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"From the anatomical position, a person raises both arms sideways until the hands meet above the head, then brings them back down to the sides. Which movements occur at the shoulder, in order?","o":["Abduction, then adduction","Adduction, then abduction","Flexion, then extension","Lateral rotation, then medial rotation","Circumduction, then extension"],"e":"Abduction = movement away from the midline; adduction = movement towards the midline. Raising the arms sideways is abduction; returning them to the sides is adduction.","w":["Reversed: moving away from the midline comes first.","Flexion moves the arm forwards, not sideways.","Rotation turns the limb about its long axis.","Circumduction combines movements in a circle."],"k":"Sideways out = abduction; back in = adduction.","rn":[["","Abduction and adduction are relative to what?","Abduction and adduction are relative to what?","A reference line; Usually the midline"],["","Adduction","Adduction","Movement towards the midline"],["","Abduction","Abduction","Movement away from the midline"]]},{"id":3024,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"With the elbow flexed at 90° and the arm at the side, a patient turns the forearm so the hand moves outwards, away from the abdomen, keeping the elbow still. Which movement is this at the shoulder?","o":["Lateral (external) rotation","Medial (internal) rotation","Abduction","Supination","Extension"],"e":"Lateral (external) rotation = rotation outwards, away from the midline; medial (internal) rotation = rotation towards the midline. With the elbow bent, rotating the arm moves the hand outwards: lateral rotation.","w":["The hand moves away from the body, so the rotation is outwards.","The arm is not lifted away from the side.","Supination turns the palm; here the whole arm rotates.","Extension moves the limb backwards in the sagittal plane."],"k":"Lateral = external rotation.","rn":[["","What is another name for lateral rotation?","What is another name for lateral rotation?","External rotation"],["","Lateral rotation","Lateral rotation","Rotation outwards; Away from the midline"]]},{"id":3025,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A patient holds a bowl of soup in the palm, then turns the hand to pour the soup out onto the floor. Which movements of the forearm occur?","o":["Supination, then pronation","Pronation, then supination","Flexion, then extension","Medial rotation, then lateral rotation","Inversion, then eversion"],"e":"Supination = palmar surface faces forwards (or up, “holding a bowl of soup”), as in the anatomical position. Pronation = the palm rotates to face backwards (or down), tipping the soup out.","w":["Holding soup needs the palm up/forwards – supination comes first.","These change the joint angle, not the palm’s direction.","These terms describe rotation of a limb, not of the palm.","Inversion/eversion are foot movements."],"k":"Soup in = supination; soup out = pronation.","rn":[["","How do you remember supination?","How do you remember supination?","Holding a bowl of soup"],["","Supination","Supination","Palm and forearm face forwards"]]},{"id":3026,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A patient walks on their heels with the forefoot lifted off the ground, then turns the soles to face each other. Which movements occur at the foot?","o":["Dorsiflexion, then inversion","Plantarflexion, then inversion","Dorsiflexion, then eversion","Plantarflexion, then eversion","Extension, then adduction"],"e":"Dorsiflexion: dorsum of the foot and toes move upwards (walking on heels). Inversion: sole faces towards the midline, foot turned inwards.","w":["Lifting the forefoot moves the dorsum upwards – dorsiflexion.","Soles facing each other means facing the midline – inversion.","Wrong on both.","These general terms are not the specific foot movements."],"k":"Heel walk = dorsiflexion; soles in = inversion.","rn":[["","Which movement turns the sole of the foot towards the midline?","Which movement turns the sole of the foot towards the midline?","Inversion"],["","Inversion","Inversion","Foot turned inwards; Sole faces the midline"],["","Dorsiflexion","Dorsiflexion","Dorsum of foot and toes; Move upwards"]]},{"id":3027,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Which movement is a <b>combination</b> of other movements in a circular motion, rather than a single movement in one plane?","o":["Circumduction","Medial rotation","Abduction","Pronation","Dorsiflexion"],"e":"Circumduction combines flexion, extension, abduction and adduction in a circular motion, so the distal end of the limb traces a circle.","w":["Rotation turns a limb about its axis.","Abduction is away from the midline only.","Pronation turns the palm backwards.","Dorsiflexion lifts the dorsum of the foot."],"k":"Circumduction = combined circular movement.","rn":[["","Circumduction","Circumduction","All movements combined; In a circle"]]},{"id":3028,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"At the wrist, a patient moves the back of the hand upwards towards the posterior forearm. This movement can be called extension. Which other term is sometimes used for it?","o":["Dorsiflexion of the hand","Plantarflexion of the hand","Supination of the hand","Abduction of the hand","Pronation of the hand"],"e":"Dorsiflexion moves the dorsum (back) upwards. At the wrist, moving the dorsum of the hand upwards (extension) is sometimes called dorsiflexion of the hand.","w":["Plantarflexion is pointing the foot to the floor.","Supination turns the palm forwards.","Abduction is away from the midline.","Pronation turns the palm backwards."],"k":"Wrist extension = dorsiflexion of the hand.","rn":[["","Which movement of the hand at the wrist is dorsiflexion?","Which movement of the hand at the wrist is dorsiflexion?","Extension at the wrist"]]},{"id":3029,"t":"Anatomy in Clinical Practice","s":"A doctor inserts a cannula into a vein, later passes a urinary catheter, and then inserts a tube into the pleural space. What do all three have in common?","o":["They are procedures that rely on anatomy","They are invasive diagnostic imaging procedures","They are performed only by surgeons","They are part of history taking","They need no anatomical knowledge"],"e":"Venepuncture and cannulation, catheterisation and chest drains are procedures whose training relies on anatomy. Anatomy underpins history, examination, investigation and management for all clinicians.","w":["Imaging-based invasive diagnostics are endoscopy and angiography.","All clinicians perform procedures such as these.","They are procedures, not history.","Each needs anatomy to be done safely."],"k":"Procedures need anatomy.","rn":[["","Which 4 procedures need anatomy training?","Which 4 procedures need anatomy training?","Venepuncture; Cannulation; Catheterisation; Chest drain"]]},{"id":3030,"t":"Anatomy in Clinical Practice","s":"A patient has a camera passed into the gut, and another has contrast injected into blood vessels to image them. Which type of procedure are these?","o":["Invasive diagnostic procedures (endoscopy, angiography)","Procedural training tasks (cannulation, chest drain)","Non-invasive imaging only","Surgical management","Physical examination"],"e":"Endoscopy and angiography are listed as invasive diagnostic procedures that depend on anatomy.","w":["Those are therapeutic or access procedures.","Both enter the body.","They are diagnostic.","They are investigations."],"k":"Endoscopy, angiography = invasive diagnostic.","rn":[["","Examples of invasive diagnostic procedures","Examples of invasive diagnostic procedures","Endoscopy; Angiography"]]},{"id":3031,"t":"What Is Anatomy","s":"A pathologist describes a tumour’s size and position by eye, then examines its cells under a microscope. Which statement is correct?","o":["The first is gross anatomy; the second is histology","The first is histology; the second is gross anatomy","Both are gross anatomy","Both are histology","The first is embryology; the second is histology"],"e":"Gross anatomy covers structures visible without magnification; microscopic anatomy (histology) covers structures visible only with magnification. Used alone, “anatomy” usually means gross anatomy.","w":["Reversed.","The second needs magnification.","The first needs no magnification.","Not development."],"k":"Naked eye = gross; microscope = histology.","rn":[["","Gross vs microscopic anatomy","Gross vs microscopic anatomy","Gross: seen without magnification; Microscopic: needs magnification"],["","Used alone, what does \"anatomy\" mean?","Used alone, what does \"anatomy\" mean?","Gross anatomy"],["","Microscopic anatomy is also called?","Microscopic anatomy is also called?","Histology"]]},{"id":3032,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A structure in the leg is described as “anterior, lateral and superficial”. Which three opposite terms would describe a structure in the opposite position?","o":["Posterior, medial and deep","Posterior, lateral and deep","Dorsal, medial and superficial","Ventral, medial and deep","Posterior, medial and distal"],"e":"Opposites: anterior/posterior (ventral/dorsal), lateral/medial, superficial/deep.","w":["Lateral must become medial.","Superficial must become deep.","Ventral = anterior.","Distal is not the opposite of superficial."],"k":"Know the opposite pairs."},{"id":3033,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"A knee MRI is viewed as a vertical slice that shows the front and back of the knee, with the femur above and the tibia below. Which plane is this?","o":["Sagittal","Coronal","Transverse","Axial","Horizontal"],"e":"A vertical plane separating right from left (sagittal) shows anterior–posterior and superior–inferior relations together. A coronal plane shows right–left (medial–lateral) and superior–inferior.","w":["Coronal would show medial and lateral sides, not front and back.","Transverse/axial is horizontal and cannot show bone above and below.","Same as transverse.","Same as transverse."],"k":"Sagittal shows front–back and up–down.","rn":[["","Sagittal plane","Sagittal plane","Vertical; Separates right and left"]]},{"id":3034,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A patient is examined lying supine with the palms facing the ceiling. A student describes the thumb as “medial”. Why is this wrong?","o":["Terms refer to the anatomical position, where the thumb is lateral","Medial is only used for the lower limb","The thumb is always posterior","Supine reverses all terms","The thumb is distal, not medial"],"e":"The anatomical position is the standard reference for describing relationships, whatever position the patient is in. It has the upper limbs by the side, palms facing forwards and thumbs out to the side, so the thumb is lateral.","w":["Medial/lateral are used throughout the body.","In the anatomical position the palm (and thumb side) faces forwards.","Position changes do not change terms.","Distal compares to the trunk, a different axis."],"k":"Always describe as if in the anatomical position.","rn":[["","Anatomical position: fingers and thumbs","Anatomical position: fingers and thumbs","Fingers straight, together; Thumbs out to side"],["","What do anatomical terms of position describe?","What do anatomical terms of position describe?","Location of structures"]]},{"id":3035,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"Which feature does NOT belong to the anatomical position?","o":["Palms facing the thighs","Standing upright","Feet together, toes pointing forwards","Top of the ear level with the bone under the rim of the eye","Fingers straight and together, thumbs out to the side"],"e":"In the anatomical position the hands face forwards (forearm supinated), not towards the thighs. Other features: upright, face forwards, mouth closed, neutral expression, feet together, fingers straight and together, thumbs out.","w":["Part of it.","Part of it.","Part of it.","Part of it."],"k":"Palms face forwards."},{"id":3036,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A patient is to have surgery on the left hip. Which position is used, and why?","o":["Right lateral recumbent, so the left hip faces up","Left lateral recumbent, so the left hip faces up","Prone, so the hip is posterior","Supine, with the hip extended","Fowler’s, sitting up"],"e":"Right lateral recumbent (lying on the right side) is used for left kidney or hip surgery, so the operated side is uppermost.","w":["Lying on the left side would put the left hip on the table.","Prone is for spinal surgery.","Not the position given.","Fowler’s is for head, neck and shoulder surgery."],"k":"Left hip → right lateral recumbent.","rn":[["","What is the right lateral recumbent position used for?","What is the right lateral recumbent position used for?","Left hip surgery; Left renal surgery"],["","What surgery uses the left lateral recumbent position?","What surgery uses the left lateral recumbent position?","Right hip surgery; Right renal surgery"],["","What are the 2 lying positions on the side?","What are the 2 lying positions on the side?","Right lateral recumbent; Left lateral recumbent"]]},{"id":3037,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A patient is placed lying on the left side with knees bent towards the chest. For which examination is this position used?","o":["Digital rectal examination","Examination of the abdomen","Spinal surgery","Shoulder surgery","Surgery on the left kidney"],"e":"Left lateral recumbent with the knees flexed is the position for digital rectal examination.","w":["Abdomen is examined supine.","Spinal surgery is prone.","Shoulder surgery is Fowler’s.","Left kidney surgery uses right lateral recumbent."],"k":"DRE: left lateral, knees flexed.","rn":[["","Which examination uses the left lateral recumbent position?","Which examination uses the left lateral recumbent position?","Digital rectal examination; With knees flexed"]]},{"id":3038,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A runner lands on the outside of the foot, so the sole turns to face the other foot. Which movement has been forced?","o":["Inversion","Eversion","Dorsiflexion","Lateral rotation","Abduction"],"e":"Inversion: the sole faces towards the midline with the foot turned inwards. Eversion is the opposite.","w":["Eversion turns the sole away from the midline.","Dorsiflexion lifts the dorsum.","Rotation is about the limb’s long axis.","Abduction moves the limb from the midline."],"k":"Sole to midline = inversion.","rn":[["","Which movement turns the sole of the foot towards the midline?","Which movement turns the sole of the foot towards the midline?","Inversion"],["","Inversion","Inversion","Foot turned inwards; Sole faces the midline"]]},{"id":3039,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Which list contains only movements that DECREASE the angle at a joint or turn a surface towards the midline?","o":["Flexion, inversion, medial rotation","Extension, eversion, lateral rotation","Flexion, eversion, abduction","Extension, inversion, adduction","Plantarflexion, eversion, lateral rotation"],"e":"Flexion decreases the angle; inversion turns the sole towards the midline; medial rotation rotates towards the midline.","w":["All the opposite.","Eversion and abduction move away from the midline.","Extension increases the angle.","Eversion and lateral rotation move away."],"k":"Flexion, inversion, medial rotation.","rn":[["","Medial rotation","Medial rotation","Rotation towards the midline; Also called internal rotation"]]},{"id":3040,"t":"Body Regions","s":"A clinical note reads: “sagittal plane through the thorax, medial to the nipple”. Which statement about this description is correct?","o":["Region, plane and term together pin down the location","The plane alone would locate the cut precisely","“Medial to the nipple” means towards the side of the body","The plane separates anterior and posterior parts","Body regions are not needed if a plane is named"],"e":"Using the correct body region gives context for terms and planes: “sagittal plane” alone could be anywhere; adding the region (thorax) and a term with a landmark (medial to the nipple) locates it.","w":["A plane needs context: many sagittal planes exist.","Medial means towards the midline.","Sagittal separates right and left.","Regions give the context."],"k":"Region + plane + term = precise.","rn":[["","Why name a body region with a plane or term?","Why name a body region with a plane or term?","Gives context"]]},{"id":3041,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"A patient has a mass between the knee and the ankle on the back of the right limb. Which description uses the correct region name?","o":["Posterior right leg","Posterior right thigh","Anterior right leg","Posterior left leg","Posterior right foot"],"e":"In anatomy the leg is the part of the lower limb between knee and ankle; the thigh is between hip and knee. Back = posterior.","w":["The thigh is between hip and knee.","It is on the back.","It is the right limb.","It is above the ankle."],"k":"Leg = knee to ankle."},{"id":3042,"t":"Body Regions","s":"In anatomical terms, which region is the “arm”?","o":["Between the shoulder and the elbow","The whole upper limb","Between the elbow and the wrist","Between the wrist and the fingertips","The shoulder only"],"e":"The free part of the upper limb is divided into arm (shoulder to elbow), elbow, forearm (elbow to wrist), wrist and hand.","w":["That is the upper limb.","That is the forearm.","That is the hand.","The shoulder is a separate region."],"k":"Arm = shoulder to elbow.","rn":[["","Which part of the upper limb lies between the shoulder and elbow?","Which part of the upper limb lies between the shoulder and elbow?","Arm"]]},{"id":3043,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"In the anatomical position, which way do the palms of the hands face?","o":["Forwards","Backwards","Towards the thighs","Upwards","Towards each other"],"e":"In the anatomical position: <b>upper limbs by side, hands face forwards</b>, fingers straight and together, thumbs out to the side.","w":["That would be pronation.","Not the anatomical position.","Not the anatomical position.","Not the anatomical position."],"k":"Anatomical position: palms face forwards.","rn":[["","Supination","Supination","Palm and forearm face forwards"],["","Anatomical position: where do the upper limbs and hands go?","Anatomical position: where do the upper limbs and hands go?","Upper limbs by the sides; Hands facing forwards"]]},{"id":3044,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"Which feature describes the head in the anatomical position?","o":["Top of the ear level with the bone under the eye rim","Top of the ear level with the top of the eyebrow","Chin level with the bone under the rim of the eye","Face forwards, chin tucked down onto the chest","Face tilted upwards, mouth slightly open"],"e":"The head: <b>face forwards, mouth closed, facial expression neutral</b>, with the <b>top of the ear at the same level as the bone under the rim of the eye</b>.","w":["The landmark is the bone under the eye rim.","The ear is the reference.","The chin is not tucked.","Mouth closed, face forwards."],"k":"Head: face forwards; ear top level with infra-orbital bone.","rn":[["","Anatomical position: what level is the top of the ear?","Anatomical position: what level is the top of the ear?","Bone under rim of eye"]]},{"id":3045,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"Which feature of the anatomical position is <b>incorrect</b>?","o":["Feet apart, toes pointing outwards","Standing upright","Upper limbs by the side","Fingers straight and together","Thumbs pointing out to the side"],"e":"In the anatomical position the <b>feet are together and the toes point forwards</b>.","w":["Correct feature.","Correct feature.","Correct feature.","Correct feature."],"k":"Feet together, toes forwards.","rn":[["","Anatomical position: how is the body held?","Anatomical position: how is the body held?","Standing upright; Feet together, toes pointing forwards"]]},{"id":3046,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"Why is the anatomical position used?","o":["It is the standard reference for describing relationships","It is the most comfortable position for examining a patient","It is the position used for most surgical procedures","It is the position a patient lies in for imaging","It is the natural resting position of the body"],"e":"The anatomical position is the <b>standard reference position</b> when describing the human body and the <b>relationship of structures to one another</b>.","w":["Not the reason.","Surgery uses various named positions.","Imaging positions vary.","Not the reason."],"k":"Anatomical position = standard reference for description.","rn":[["","What is the anatomical position?","What is the anatomical position?","Standard reference position; Used to describe the body; And how structures relate"]]},{"id":3047,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Why is the forearm described as “supinated” in the anatomical position?","o":["The hand faces forwards, like holding a bowl of soup","The hand faces backwards, like lying prone","The palm faces the side of the thigh","The thumb points towards the body","The hand faces forwards, like pushing open a door"],"e":"In the anatomical position the hand faces forwards and the <b>forearm is in supination</b>: “<b>it's like holding a bowl of soup</b>”.","w":["That would be pronation.","Palms face forwards.","Thumbs point out to the side.","The notes used a bowl of soup."],"k":"Anatomical position: forearm supinated (“bowl of soup”).","rn":[["","How do you remember supination?","How do you remember supination?","Holding a bowl of soup"]]},{"id":3048,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A patient lies face up on the operating table for an abdominal operation. Name the position.","e":"Supine = lying on the back, used e.g. for examining the abdomen.","k":"Supine.","a":["supine"],"rn":[["","What is the supine position used for?","What is the supine position used for?","Abdominal examination; Lower limb examination"],["","What are the 2 lying positions on the front or back?","What are the 2 lying positions on the front or back?","Prone; Supine"]]},{"id":3049,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A doctor is about to examine a patient's abdomen. In which position is the patient usually placed?","o":["Supine","Prone","Right lateral recumbent","Fowler's","Trendelenburg"],"e":"<b>Supine</b> is the usual position for <b>abdominal examination and lower limb examination</b>.","w":["Back examination and spinal surgery.","Left hip or left renal surgery.","Shoulder, head and neck surgery.","Fainting and some surgery."],"k":"Supine: abdominal and lower-limb examination.","rn":[["","What is the supine position used for?","What is the supine position used for?","Abdominal examination; Lower limb examination"]]},{"id":3050,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A patient needs a digital rectal examination. Which position is used?","o":["Left lateral recumbent, knees flexed","Right lateral recumbent, knees flexed","Left lateral recumbent, knees extended","Prone, knees extended","Supine, legs straight"],"e":"<b>Left lateral recumbent</b> is used for right hip, right renal surgery and <b>digital rectal examination (with knees flexed)</b>.","w":["Right lateral is for left hip/renal surgery.","Knees are flexed.","Not the listed position.","Not the listed position."],"k":"DRE: left lateral recumbent, knees flexed.","rn":[["","Which examination uses the left lateral recumbent position?","Which examination uses the left lateral recumbent position?","Digital rectal examination; With knees flexed"]]},{"id":3051,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A surgeon is operating on a patient's <b>left</b> kidney. Which position is most appropriate?","o":["Right lateral recumbent","Left lateral recumbent","Prone","Supine","Trendelenburg"],"e":"<b>Right lateral recumbent</b> (lying on the right side) is used for <b>left hip and left renal surgery</b>. Left lateral recumbent is used for the right side.","w":["For right hip/right renal surgery.","Spinal surgery.","Abdominal examination.","Fainting, lower limb and gynaecological surgery."],"k":"Right lateral recumbent → left hip/renal surgery.","rn":[["","What is the right lateral recumbent position used for?","What is the right lateral recumbent position used for?","Left hip surgery; Left renal surgery"],["","What surgery uses the left lateral recumbent position?","What surgery uses the left lateral recumbent position?","Right hip surgery; Right renal surgery"],["","What are the 2 lying positions on the side?","What are the 2 lying positions on the side?","Right lateral recumbent; Left lateral recumbent"]]},{"id":3052,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A patient who has fainted is laid on their back with the head end tilted down. Name this position.","e":"Trendelenburg: supine with the head end lowered, used for patients who have fainted and (with lithotomy) gynaecological surgery.","k":"Trendelenburg.","a":["trendelenburg","trendelenberg","trendelenburg position"],"rn":[["","What is the Trendelenburg position used for?","What is the Trendelenburg position used for?","Patients who have fainted; Lower limb surgery; Gynaecological surgery (lithotomy)"]]},{"id":3053,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A patient is positioned for shoulder surgery. Which named position is used?","o":["Fowler's","Trendelenburg","Prone","Left lateral recumbent","Supine"],"e":"<b>Fowler's</b> position is used <b>for shoulder surgery and head and neck surgery</b>.","w":["Fainting, lower-limb, gynae surgery.","Spinal surgery.","Right hip/renal, DRE.","Abdominal/lower-limb examination."],"k":"Fowler's: shoulder, head and neck surgery.","rn":[["","What is the Fowler's position used for?","What is the Fowler's position used for?","Shoulder surgery; Head and neck surgery"]]},{"id":3054,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"For gynaecological surgery in the Trendelenburg position, how are the lower limbs placed?","o":["In a lithotomy position","Fully extended and together","Flexed with the patient on the left side","Abducted with the patient prone","In the anatomical position"],"e":"Trendelenburg is used for <b>gynaecological surgery (with lower limbs in a lithotomy position)</b>.","w":["Not stated.","That is left lateral recumbent with knees flexed (DRE).","Not stated.","Not stated."],"k":"Gynae surgery: Trendelenburg + lithotomy.","rn":[["","What is the Trendelenburg position used for?","What is the Trendelenburg position used for?","Patients who have fainted; Lower limb surgery; Gynaecological surgery (lithotomy)"]]},{"id":3055,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"A vertical plane divides the body into front and back parts. Which plane is this?","o":["Coronal","Sagittal","Median","Transverse","Axial"],"e":"The <b>coronal</b> plane is <b>vertical, separating anterior and posterior</b> parts.","w":["Separates right and left.","Midline sagittal plane.","Separates superior and inferior.","Another name for transverse."],"k":"Coronal = anterior vs posterior.","rn":[["","Coronal plane","Coronal plane","Vertical; Separates anterior and posterior"]]},{"id":3056,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"A vertical plane passes exactly through the midline, dividing the body into equal right and left halves. What is it called?","o":["Median (midsagittal)","Coronal plane","Transverse plane","Parasagittal plane","Horizontal plane"],"e":"The <b>midsagittal or median</b> plane is the sagittal plane in the <b>midline</b>, splitting the body into <b>equal</b> right and left halves.","w":["Anterior/posterior.","Superior/inferior.","Parasagittal planes are off the midline.","Superior/inferior."],"k":"Median = midsagittal = midline.","rn":[["","Midsagittal (median) plane","Midsagittal (median) plane","Down the midline; Equal right and left halves"],["","Which plane splits the body into equal right and left halves?","Which plane splits the body into equal right and left halves?","Midsagittal (median)"]]},{"id":3057,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"Which three names are used for the plane that separates superior from inferior parts?","o":["Transverse, axial or horizontal","Coronal, frontal or vertical","Sagittal, median or midline","Transverse, coronal or axial","Horizontal, frontal or axial"],"e":"The <b>transverse, axial or horizontal</b> plane separates <b>superior and inferior</b>.","w":["Coronal separates anterior/posterior.","Sagittal separates right/left.","Coronal is not a synonym.","Frontal = coronal."],"k":"Transverse = axial = horizontal.","rn":[["","Transverse plane","Transverse plane","Also axial or horizontal; Separates superior and inferior"],["","Which plane separates superior from inferior?","Which plane separates superior from inferior?","Transverse (axial / horizontal)"],["","What are 2 other names for the transverse plane?","What are 2 other names for the transverse plane?","Axial; Horizontal"]]},{"id":3058,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"A sagittal plane is placed 5 cm to the right of the midline. Which statement is correct?","o":["It separates right and left, but not equally","It separates right and left parts into equal halves","It separates anterior and posterior parts","It separates superior and inferior parts","It is a coronal plane offset from the midline"],"e":"A <b>sagittal</b> plane is vertical and separates <b>right and left</b>. Only the <b>median/midsagittal</b> plane passes through the midline giving <b>equal</b> halves.","w":["Only the median plane gives equal halves.","That is coronal.","That is transverse.","It is sagittal."],"k":"Sagittal: right/left; only the median plane gives equal halves.","rn":[["","Sagittal plane","Sagittal plane","Vertical; Separates right and left"],["","Midsagittal (median) plane","Midsagittal (median) plane","Down the midline; Equal right and left halves"],["","Which plane splits the body into equal right and left halves?","Which plane splits the body into equal right and left halves?","Midsagittal (median)"]]},{"id":3059,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"A scan shows a “slice” of the abdomen with the front of the body at the top of the image and the back at the bottom. Which plane is shown?","o":["Transverse (axial)","Coronal","Sagittal","Median","Parasagittal"],"e":"A slice that separates <b>superior from inferior</b> parts, showing front and back and left and right in one image, is in the <b>transverse/axial/horizontal</b> plane.","w":["Coronal shows superior/inferior and left/right.","Sagittal shows front/back and up/down.","A sagittal plane.","A sagittal plane."],"k":"Axial slice shows anterior–posterior and left–right.","rn":[["","Transverse plane","Transverse plane","Also axial or horizontal; Separates superior and inferior"],["","Which plane separates superior from inferior?","Which plane separates superior from inferior?","Transverse (axial / horizontal)"],["","What are 2 other names for the transverse plane?","What are 2 other names for the transverse plane?","Axial; Horizontal"]]},{"id":3060,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"On a coronal section through the body, which pair of directions can be seen in the same image?","o":["Superior–inferior and right–left","Anterior–posterior and superior–inferior","Anterior–posterior and right–left","Proximal–distal only","Superficial–deep only"],"e":"The coronal plane is <b>vertical</b> and separates <b>anterior from posterior</b>; the cut surface shows <b>superior–inferior</b> and <b>right–left</b>.","w":["That is a sagittal section.","That is a transverse section.","Not specific to a plane.","Not specific to a plane."],"k":"Coronal section: up–down and left–right visible.","rn":[["","Sagittal plane","Sagittal plane","Vertical; Separates right and left"],["","Superior and inferior are relative to what?","Superior and inferior are relative to what?","Vertical axis of body"],["","Midsagittal (median) plane","Midsagittal (median) plane","Down the midline; Equal right and left halves"]]},{"id":3061,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"Structure A lies closer to the surface of the body than structure B. Which term describes A relative to B?","o":["Superficial","Deep","Proximal","Medial","Anterior"],"e":"<b>Superficial</b> = closer to the surface; <b>deep</b> = further away from the surface. These are relative to the <b>surface of the body</b>.","w":["Further from the surface.","Relates to the trunk/origin.","Relates to the midline.","Relates to the front."],"k":"Superficial = nearer the surface.","rn":[["","Superficial","Superficial","closer to the surface"],["","Superficial and deep are relative to what?","Superficial and deep are relative to what?","The surface of the body"]]},{"id":3062,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"Medial and lateral are described relative to which reference?","o":["The median sagittal plane","The surface of the body","The vertical axis of the body","A structure's origin","The front and back of the body"],"e":"<b>Medial / lateral</b> are relative to the <b>median sagittal plane</b>. Superficial/deep relate to the surface; superior/inferior to the vertical axis; proximal/distal to a structure's origin.","w":["Superficial/deep.","Superior/inferior.","Proximal/distal.","Anterior/posterior."],"k":"Medial/lateral → median sagittal plane.","rn":[["","Medial and lateral are relative to what?","Medial and lateral are relative to what?","The median sagittal plane"]]},{"id":3063,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"The wrist is described as distal to the elbow. What does “distal” mean here?","o":["Further from the trunk or the structure’s origin","Closer to the trunk or the structure’s origin","Further from the median sagittal plane","Further from the surface of the body","Further from the head along the vertical axis"],"e":"<b>Distal</b> = further from the trunk or origin; <b>proximal</b> = closer to the trunk or origin. The notes' example: <b>wrist distal to elbow</b>.","w":["That is proximal.","That is lateral.","That is deep.","That is inferior."],"k":"Distal = further from trunk/origin.","rn":[["","Distal","Distal","Further from trunk or origin"]]},{"id":3064,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"Which pairs of anatomical terms are synonyms?","o":["Anterior = ventral; posterior = dorsal","Anterior = dorsal; posterior = ventral","Superior = caudal; inferior = cranial","Medial = proximal; lateral = distal","Superficial = anterior; deep = posterior"],"e":"The slide lists <b>anterior (ventral)</b> and <b>posterior (dorsal)</b>.","w":["Reversed.","Cranial ≈ superior; caudal ≈ inferior.","Different reference points.","Different reference points."],"k":"Anterior = ventral; posterior = dorsal.","rn":[["","Posterior (dorsal)","Posterior (dorsal)","towards the back"],["","Anterior (ventral)","Anterior (ventral)","towards the front"],["","Anterior and posterior are relative to what?","Anterior and posterior are relative to what?","Front and back of body"]]},{"id":3065,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"What do the terms cranial and caudal mean, and how can they be used?","o":["Towards the head and towards the tail; can replace superior and inferior","Towards the tail and towards the head; can replace superior and inferior","Towards the head and towards the tail; can replace anterior and posterior","Towards the front and towards the back; can replace ventral and dorsal","Towards the head and towards the tail; can replace proximal and distal"],"e":"<b>Cranial</b> = towards the head; <b>caudal</b> = towards the tail. They <b>may be used instead of superior and inferior</b>.","w":["Reversed.","They replace superior/inferior.","Not the meaning.","They replace superior/inferior."],"k":"Cranial/caudal can replace superior/inferior.","rn":[["","Which terms may replace superior and inferior?","Which terms may replace superior and inferior?","Cranial and caudal"]]},{"id":3066,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"In which context is the term “rostral” used?","o":["In embryology, in the head, relative to the nose","In embryology, in the limbs, relative to the trunk","In the thorax, relative to the midline","In imaging, to describe the axial plane","In the head, relative to the back of the skull"],"e":"<b>Rostral</b> is sometimes used <b>in embryology in the head</b> to describe the position of a structure <b>relative to the nose</b>.","w":["Rostral relates to the nose.","Not the context.","Not the context.","Relative to the nose."],"k":"Rostral: embryology, head, relative to nose.","rn":[["","Where is \"rostral\" sometimes used?","Where is \"rostral\" sometimes used?","In embryology; In the head"]]},{"id":3067,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"A lesion on the left causes a sign on the right. What single term describes the sign’s side relative to the lesion?","e":"Contralateral = opposite side; ipsilateral = same side.","k":"Contralateral.","a":["contralateral"],"rn":[["","Contralateral","Contralateral","opposite side"],["","Which pairs of terms describe side?","Which pairs of terms describe side?","Right or left; Ipsilateral or contralateral"]]},{"id":3068,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"A lesion on the right side of the body causes a sign on the left side. How would the sign be described relative to the lesion?","o":["Contralateral","Ipsilateral","Medial","Proximal","Superficial"],"e":"<b>Contralateral</b> describes the <b>opposite side</b>; ipsilateral the same side.","w":["Same side.","Towards the midline.","Closer to the trunk.","Closer to the surface."],"k":"Contralateral = opposite side.","rn":[["","Contralateral","Contralateral","opposite side"]]},{"id":3069,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"Superior and inferior are described relative to which reference?","o":["The vertical axis of the body","The median sagittal plane","The surface of the body","A structure’s origin or the trunk","The front and back of the body"],"e":"<b>Superior / inferior</b> are relative to the <b>vertical axis</b> of the body: superior = towards the head or top; inferior = towards the feet or bottom.","w":["That is for medial/lateral.","That is for superficial/deep.","That is for proximal/distal.","That is for anterior/posterior."],"k":"Superior/inferior → vertical axis.","rn":[["","Superior and inferior are relative to what?","Superior and inferior are relative to what?","Vertical axis of body"]]},{"id":3070,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"Point P is nearer the midline than point Q. Point Q is nearer the feet than point P. Which description of P relative to Q is correct?","o":["Medial and superior","Lateral and inferior","Medial and inferior","Lateral and superior","Proximal and deep"],"e":"Nearer the midline = <b>medial</b>. If Q is nearer the feet, P is nearer the head = <b>superior</b>.","w":["Both reversed.","P is superior.","P is medial.","Depth and trunk are not described."],"k":"Combine terms: medial (midline) + superior (head end).","rn":[["","Medial","Medial","towards the midline"],["","Superior","Superior","towards the head or top"]]},{"id":3071,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"A structure lies towards the front of the body. Which term describes its position?","o":["Anterior","Posterior","Cranial","Lateral","Superficial"],"e":"<b>Anterior</b> = towards the front of the body; <b>posterior</b> = towards the back.","w":["Back.","Head.","Away from the midline.","Nearer the surface."],"k":"Anterior = front.","rn":[["","Anterior and posterior are relative to what?","Anterior and posterior are relative to what?","Front and back of body"],["","Anterior (ventral)","Anterior (ventral)","towards the front"]]},{"id":3072,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"Which list contains only <b>paired opposite</b> terms of location?","o":["Superficial/deep, proximal/distal, ipsilateral/contralateral","Superficial/deep, proximal/distal, flexion/extension","Superficial/deep, prone/supine, ipsilateral/contralateral","Coronal/sagittal, proximal/distal, ipsilateral/contralateral","Superficial/deep, proximal/distal, supination/pronation"],"e":"Location terms come in pairs: superficial/deep, anterior/posterior, cranial/caudal, medial/lateral, superior/inferior, proximal/distal, right/left, ipsilateral/contralateral.","w":["Flexion/extension is movement.","Prone/supine are positions.","Coronal/sagittal are planes.","Supination/pronation is movement."],"k":"Location terms come in opposite pairs.","rn":[["","Which pairs of terms describe side?","Which pairs of terms describe side?","Right or left; Ipsilateral or contralateral"]]},{"id":3073,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"Why should body regions be combined with planes and terms when describing a location?","o":["It gives accurate descriptions with context","It removes the need to use the anatomical position","It removes the need to name a plane at all","It is required only in surgical operation notes","It lets structures be described without terms"],"e":"<b>Using the correct body regions gives context</b> for terms/planes; combining regions, planes and terms gives accurate descriptions.","w":["The anatomical position is still the reference.","Planes are still used.","Not stated.","Terms are still used."],"k":"Regions + planes + terms = accurate description.","rn":[["","Why combine body regions with planes and terms?","Why combine body regions with planes and terms?","Gives accurate descriptions"]]},{"id":3074,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"Which description correctly locates a horizontal cut through the lower limb just above the ankle on the left?","o":["A transverse plane through the left leg, just proximal to the ankle","A transverse plane through the left leg, just distal to the knee","A coronal plane through the left leg, just proximal to the ankle","A sagittal plane through the thorax, lateral to the nipple","A transverse plane through the thorax, medial to the nipple"],"e":"Examples: “<b>transverse plane through the left leg, just proximal to the ankle</b>” and “<b>sagittal plane through the thorax, medial to the nipple</b>”.","w":["The example was proximal to the ankle.","The example was transverse.","The thorax example was sagittal, medial to the nipple.","The thorax example was sagittal."],"k":"Example: transverse plane through left leg, just proximal to ankle.","rn":[["","What is an example of combining regions, planes and terms?","What is an example of combining regions, planes and terms?","Transverse plane through left leg; Just proximal to the ankle"],["","Which plane would pass through the left leg just proximal to the ankle?","Which plane would pass through the left leg just proximal to the ankle?","Transverse plane"]]},{"id":3075,"t":"Body Regions","s":"Beyond body regions, which landmarks are used to describe locations more precisely?","o":["Surface landmarks, bones, muscles, vessels, nerves","Planes alone, without body regions","Named positions such as supine and prone","Terms of movement such as flexion","Imaging coordinates from CT scans"],"e":"As you progress, instead of body regions you might use <b>surface landmarks, bones, muscles or even vessels and nerves</b>.","w":["Regions give context to planes.","Positions are not location descriptors.","Movement terms describe motion.","Not stated."],"k":"Refine descriptions with landmarks, bones, muscles, vessels, nerves.","rn":[["","As your anatomy study progresses, what can replace body regions in descriptions?","As your anatomy study progresses, what can replace body regions in descriptions?","Surface landmarks; Bones, muscles; Vessels and nerves"]]},{"id":3076,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"A note reads “a sagittal plane through the thorax, medial to the nipple”. Which statement about this plane is correct?","o":["It separates right and left, closer to the midline than the nipple","It separates right and left, further from the midline than the nipple","It separates anterior and posterior, closer to the midline than the nipple","It separates superior and inferior at the level of the nipple","It is the median plane passing through the sternum"],"e":"A <b>sagittal</b> plane separates right and left. <b>Medial</b> to the nipple means <b>closer to the midline</b> than the nipple. It is not necessarily the median plane.","w":["Medial means closer to the midline.","That is coronal.","That is transverse.","It is not necessarily median."],"k":"Apply plane + term together.","rn":[["","Sagittal plane","Sagittal plane","Vertical; Separates right and left"],["","Midsagittal (median) plane","Midsagittal (median) plane","Down the midline; Equal right and left halves"]]},{"id":3077,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A patient bends their elbow so the forearm moves closer to the arm. Which movement is this?","o":["Flexion","Extension","Abduction","Supination","Circumduction"],"e":"<b>Flexion decreases the angle at the joint</b>; extension increases it. The notes also suggest flexion moves towards the fetal position, “all curled up in a ball”.","w":["Increases the angle.","Away from the midline.","Forearm rotation.","Circular combination."],"k":"Flexion = decreases joint angle.","rn":[["","Flexion","Flexion","Decreases the joint angle"]]},{"id":3078,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Which movement increases the angle at a joint?","o":["Extension","Flexion","Adduction","Medial rotation","Inversion"],"e":"<b>Extension increases the angle at the joint</b>; flexion decreases it.","w":["Decreases.","Towards the midline.","Rotation towards the midline.","Sole faces medially."],"k":"Extension = increases joint angle.","rn":[["","Extension","Extension","Increases the joint angle"]]},{"id":3079,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A patient raises their arm out to the side, away from the body. Which movement is this?","o":["Abduction","Adduction","Lateral rotation","Flexion","Circumduction"],"e":"<b>Abduction</b> is movement <b>away from the midline</b> (the reference line). Adduction is towards it.","w":["Towards the midline.","Rotation outwards.","Decreases the angle.","Circular movement."],"k":"Abduction = away from midline.","rn":[["","Abduction","Abduction","Movement away from the midline"]]},{"id":3080,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Which term is another name for lateral rotation?","o":["External rotation","Internal rotation","Abduction","Eversion","Supination of the foot"],"e":"<b>Lateral rotation = external rotation</b>; <b>medial rotation = internal rotation</b>.","w":["= medial rotation.","Not a rotation.","Foot movement.","Not stated."],"k":"Lateral = external rotation; medial = internal rotation.","rn":[["","What is another name for lateral rotation?","What is another name for lateral rotation?","External rotation"]]},{"id":3081,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A physiotherapist turns a patient's limb so it rotates towards the midline. What is this movement called?","o":["Medial (internal) rotation","Lateral (external) rotation","Adduction","Pronation","Inversion"],"e":"<b>Medial rotation</b> is rotation towards the midline, also called <b>internal rotation</b>.","w":["Rotation away from midline.","Movement, not rotation, towards the midline.","Forearm-specific.","Foot-specific."],"k":"Medial rotation = internal rotation (towards midline).","rn":[["","Medial rotation","Medial rotation","Rotation towards the midline; Also called internal rotation"]]},{"id":3082,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Name the movement in which the distal end of a limb traces a circle while the proximal end stays fixed.","e":"Circumduction combines movements in a circular motion.","k":"Circumduction.","a":["circumduction"],"rn":[["","Circumduction","Circumduction","All movements combined; In a circle"],["","In circumduction, what do the two ends do?","In circumduction, what do the two ends do?","Distal end draws a circle; Proximal end stays fixed"]]},{"id":3083,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A patient walks on their heels. Name the movement at the ankle that lifts the forefoot.","e":"Dorsiflexion: the dorsum of the foot and toes move upwards.","k":"Dorsiflexion.","a":["dorsiflexion","dorsi flexion","dorsiflex"],"rn":[["","Dorsiflexion","Dorsiflexion","Dorsum of foot and toes; Move upwards"]]},{"id":3084,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A ballet dancer stands on tiptoe, pointing the sole and toes towards the floor. Which movement is occurring at the foot?","o":["Plantarflexion","Dorsiflexion","Inversion","Eversion","Abduction"],"e":"<b>Plantarflexion</b>: the <b>sole and toes point towards the floor</b>.","w":["Foot moves upwards.","Sole faces the midline.","Sole faces away.","Not a foot term used here."],"k":"Plantarflexion = sole and toes towards floor.","rn":[["","Plantarflexion","Plantarflexion","Sole and toes; Point towards the floor"]]},{"id":3085,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A patient turns the foot inwards so that the sole faces the midline. Which movement is this?","o":["Inversion","Eversion","Dorsiflexion","Plantarflexion","Medial rotation of the hip"],"e":"<b>Inversion</b>: the <b>sole faces towards the midline</b> with the foot turned inwards. Eversion is the opposite.","w":["Sole faces away from midline.","Foot moves upwards.","Sole points down.","Not a foot movement."],"k":"Inversion = sole faces midline.","rn":[["","Inversion","Inversion","Foot turned inwards; Sole faces the midline"],["","Which movement turns the sole of the foot towards the midline?","Which movement turns the sole of the foot towards the midline?","Inversion"]]},{"id":3086,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Name the foot movement in which the sole turns to face away from the midline.","e":"Eversion: sole faces away from the midline with the foot turned outwards.","k":"Eversion.","a":["eversion","evert"],"rn":[["","Which movement turns the sole of the foot away from the midline?","Which movement turns the sole of the foot away from the midline?","Eversion"],["","Eversion","Eversion","Foot turned outwards; Sole faces away from midline"]]},{"id":3087,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A patient rotates the forearm so that the palm, which was facing forwards, now faces backwards. Which movement has occurred?","o":["Pronation","Supination","Medial rotation of the arm above the elbow","Extension","Adduction"],"e":"<b>Pronation</b>: the palmar surface of the hand and forearm <b>rotate and face backwards</b>. It is the forearm rotating, not the arm above the elbow (“like the hands are lying prone”).","w":["Palm faces forwards.","The notes stress it is the forearm, not the arm above the elbow.","Changes the joint angle.","Towards the midline."],"k":"Pronation = palm faces backwards (forearm rotation).","rn":[["","Pronation","Pronation","Palm and forearm rotate; To face backwards"]]},{"id":3088,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Which movement returns the forearm to the position it has in the anatomical position?","o":["Supination","Pronation","Flexion","Eversion","Abduction"],"e":"In the anatomical position the hand faces forwards and the forearm is <b>supinated</b>: the palmar surface of the hand and forearm face forwards.","w":["Palm faces backwards.","Not related.","Foot.","Not related."],"k":"Supination = palm forwards (anatomical position).","rn":[["","Supination","Supination","Palm and forearm face forwards"],["","Which forearm position is the anatomical position?","Which forearm position is the anatomical position?","Supination"]]},{"id":3089,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Dorsiflexion can also describe which movement of the hand?","o":["Extension of the hand at the wrist (moving the hand upwards)","Flexion of the hand at the wrist (moving the hand downwards)","Extension of the fingers at the knuckles","Pronation of the forearm and hand","Abduction of the hand at the wrist"],"e":"The notes describe <b>dorsiflexion</b> as movement of the foot <b>or hand</b> upwards: <b>extension of the hand at the wrist joint</b>.","w":["That is the opposite.","The notes referred to the wrist.","Pronation is rotation.","Not the meaning."],"k":"Dorsiflexion of hand = extension at the wrist.","rn":[["","Which movement of the hand at the wrist is dorsiflexion?","Which movement of the hand at the wrist is dorsiflexion?","Extension at the wrist"]]},{"id":3090,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Abduction and adduction are movements relative to which reference line?","o":["The midline","The vertical axis of the limb","The surface of the body","The floor","The nose"],"e":"Abduction is movement of the part away from the <b>reference line (usually midline)</b>; adduction is towards it.","w":["Not stated.","Superficial/deep reference.","Not stated.","Rostral reference in embryology."],"k":"Reference line for ab/adduction: usually midline.","rn":[["","Abduction and adduction are relative to what?","Abduction and adduction are relative to what?","A reference line; Usually the midline"]]},{"id":3091,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Which image best describes flexion of the whole body?","o":["Moving towards the fetal position, curled up in a ball","Moving the limb away from the midline","Straightening the joint as if standing to attention","Turning the palm to face backwards","Pointing the toes towards the floor"],"e":"“You could also think about <b>flexion</b>, moving the joint more towards the <b>fetal position – all curled up in a ball</b>.”","w":["That is abduction.","That is extension.","That is pronation.","That is plantarflexion."],"k":"Flexion ≈ curling into the fetal position.","rn":[["","What position does flexion move towards?","What position does flexion move towards?","The fetal position (curled up)"]]},{"id":3092,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Which pair of movements are opposites?","o":["Inversion and eversion","Flexion and abduction","Pronation and plantarflexion","Dorsiflexion and eversion","Circumduction and supination"],"e":"<b>Inversion</b> (sole faces midline) and <b>eversion</b> (sole faces away) are opposites. Other opposite pairs: flexion/extension, abduction/adduction, medial/lateral rotation, dorsiflexion/plantarflexion, supination/pronation.","w":["Not opposites.","Not opposites.","Not opposites.","Not opposites."],"k":"Know opposite movement pairs.","rn":[["","Which movement turns the sole of the foot away from the midline?","Which movement turns the sole of the foot away from the midline?","Eversion"],["","Which movement turns the sole of the foot towards the midline?","Which movement turns the sole of the foot towards the midline?","Inversion"],["","Eversion","Eversion","Foot turned outwards; Sole faces away from midline"]]},{"id":3093,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A raised arm is brought back to the side of the body. Name the movement at the shoulder.","e":"Adduction: movement towards the midline.","k":"Adduction.","a":["adduction","adduct"],"rn":[["","Adduction","Adduction","Movement towards the midline"]]},{"id":3094,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Which statement about circumduction is correct?","o":["It combines several movements in a circular motion","It turns a limb around its own long axis","It moves the distal end while the proximal end also moves","It decreases the angle between two bones","It occurs only at the ankle joint"],"e":"<b>Circumduction</b> is a <b>combination of all movements in a circular motion</b>.","w":["That is rotation.","The proximal end is fixed.","That is flexion.","It occurs at many joints."],"k":"Circumduction = combined circular movement.","rn":[["","Circumduction","Circumduction","All movements combined; In a circle"]]},{"id":3095,"t":"Anatomy in Clinical Practice","s":"Why is anatomy described as underpinning the work of all clinicians, not only surgeons and radiologists?","o":["It underpins history, examination, investigation and management","It is needed only to pass written exams","It replaces the need for physiology","It is used only during procedures","It is mainly needed by dentists"],"e":"Anatomy underpins all clinical activities – history taking, examination, investigation and management plan – as well as procedural and subspecialty training.","w":["Not just exams.","Not stated.","All activities, not just procedures.","All clinicians."],"k":"Anatomy underpins all clinical work.","rn":[["","Which clinical activities does anatomy underpin?","Which clinical activities does anatomy underpin?","History taking; Investigation; Management plan; Procedural training"]]},{"id":3096,"t":"What Is Anatomy","s":"Anatomy is linked to dissection because of the origin of its name. What does the Greek root mean?","e":"Anatomy comes from the Greek temnein, meaning “to cut” – hence the link with dissection.","k":"Temnein = to cut.","a":["to cut","cut","cutting"],"rn":[["","What does the Greek \"temnein\" mean?","What does the Greek \"temnein\" mean?","\"To cut\""],["","Why is anatomy linked to dissection?","Why is anatomy linked to dissection?","Its name means \"to cut\""]]},{"id":3097,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"What single term describes a structure that is on the same side of the body as another structure?","e":"Ipsilateral = same side; contralateral = opposite side.","k":"Ipsilateral.","a":["ipsilateral"],"rn":[["","Ipsilateral","Ipsilateral","same side"]]},{"id":3098,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"A patient in the anatomical position has a mark on the front of the forearm near the wrist. Which description is most accurate?","o":["Anterior forearm, distal to the elbow","Posterior forearm, proximal to the wrist","Anterior forearm, proximal to the elbow","Lateral forearm, superior to the elbow","Posterior forearm, distal to the wrist"],"e":"In the anatomical position the palm and front of the forearm face forwards, so the mark is <b>anterior</b>. Near the wrist means <b>distal</b> to the elbow (the wrist is distal to the elbow).","w":["The front is anterior.","Near the wrist is distal to the elbow.","Not the lateral or superior position.","Distal to the wrist is the hand."],"k":"Use anatomical position + proximal/distal.","rn":[["","The wrist is Distal to the elbow","The wrist is Distal to the elbow",""],["","What is an example of distal?","What is an example of distal?","Wrist is distal to elbow"]]},{"id":3099,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"A surgeon wants to examine a patient's back. Which position and which plane would most directly separate the back of the trunk from the front?","o":["Prone; coronal plane","Supine; sagittal plane","Prone; transverse plane","Fowler's; coronal plane","Supine; median plane"],"e":"<b>Prone</b> is used to examine the back. The <b>coronal</b> plane separates <b>anterior from posterior</b>.","w":["Supine is for abdominal exam; sagittal separates right/left.","Transverse separates superior/inferior.","Fowler's is for shoulder/head and neck surgery.","Median separates right/left."],"k":"Prone + coronal (front vs back).","rn":[["","Which plane separates front from back?","Which plane separates front from back?","Coronal"]]},{"id":3100,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"Which of the following is a correct pairing of term and reference point?","o":["Proximal/distal – a structure’s origin or the trunk","Medial/lateral – the surface of the body","Superficial/deep – the vertical axis of the body","Superior/inferior – the median sagittal plane","Proximal/distal – the median sagittal plane"],"e":"From the notes: superficial/deep → surface; anterior/posterior → front and back; medial/lateral → median sagittal plane; superior/inferior → vertical axis; <b>proximal/distal → origin</b> (or trunk).","w":["Medial/lateral → median sagittal plane.","Superficial/deep → surface.","Superior/inferior → vertical axis.","Proximal/distal → origin/trunk."],"k":"Match each term pair with its reference.","rn":[["","Proximal and distal are relative to what?","Proximal and distal are relative to what?","A structure's origin"]]},{"id":3101,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A note documents: “left hip surgery – patient positioned right lateral recumbent”. A colleague argues it should be left lateral recumbent. Who is correct?","o":["The doctor: right lateral recumbent is for left hip surgery","The student: left lateral recumbent is for left hip surgery","Neither: prone is used for hip surgery","Neither: supine is used for hip surgery","Both: either side is listed for hip surgery"],"e":"The notes: <b>right lateral recumbent – for left hip, left renal surgery</b>; <b>left lateral recumbent – for right hip, right renal surgery</b> and DRE.","w":["Left lateral recumbent is for the right side.","Not listed.","Not listed.","Only one is listed for each side."],"k":"Lie on the opposite side to the operated hip/kidney.","rn":[["","What is the right lateral recumbent position used for?","What is the right lateral recumbent position used for?","Left hip surgery; Left renal surgery"],["","What surgery uses the left lateral recumbent position?","What surgery uses the left lateral recumbent position?","Right hip surgery; Right renal surgery"]]},{"id":3102,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A patient lies flat on their back for an examination of the lower limbs. Which position is this, and which named position would be used instead to examine the back?","o":["Supine; prone","Prone; supine","Supine; Fowler's","Trendelenburg; prone","Fowler's; supine"],"e":"<b>Supine</b> (on the back) is used for abdominal and lower-limb examination; <b>prone</b> is used to examine the back.","w":["Reversed.","Fowler's is for shoulder/head-neck surgery.","Trendelenburg is for fainting/surgery.","Not correct."],"k":"Supine for abdomen/lower limb; prone for back.","rn":[["","What are the 2 lying positions on the front or back?","What are the 2 lying positions on the front or back?","Prone; Supine"],["","What is the supine position used for?","What is the supine position used for?","Abdominal examination; Lower limb examination"]]},{"id":3103,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"From the anatomical position, a person turns their hands so their palms face backwards and then bends their elbows. Which movements have occurred, in order?","o":["Pronation, then flexion","Supination, then extension","Pronation, then extension","Supination, then flexion","Medial rotation, then abduction"],"e":"Palms turning to face backwards = <b>pronation</b>. Bending the elbow decreases the joint angle = <b>flexion</b>.","w":["Palms backwards is pronation; bending is flexion.","Bending is flexion.","Palms backwards is pronation.","Not the described movements."],"k":"Identify movements in sequence.","rn":[["","Flexion","Flexion","Decreases the joint angle"],["","Pronation","Pronation","Palm and forearm rotate; To face backwards"],["","In pronation, which way does the hand face?","In pronation, which way does the hand face?","Backwards"]]},{"id":3104,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"Which statement about the transverse plane is correct?","o":["Also called axial or horizontal; separates superior from inferior","Also called frontal or coronal; separates anterior from posterior","Also called axial or horizontal; separates anterior from posterior","Also called median; separates right from left","Also called axial or sagittal; separates superior from inferior"],"e":"<b>Transverse, axial or horizontal</b>: separating <b>superior and inferior</b>. (The speaker notes say “superior and posterior”, but the slide says superior and inferior.)","w":["That is coronal.","Transverse separates superior/inferior.","That is sagittal.","Sagittal is not a synonym."],"k":"Transverse = superior/inferior.","rn":[["","Transverse plane","Transverse plane","Also axial or horizontal; Separates superior and inferior"],["","Which plane separates superior from inferior?","Which plane separates superior from inferior?","Transverse (axial / horizontal)"]]},{"id":3105,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"A structure in the thorax is described as “cranial” to another. Which equivalent term could be used?","o":["Superior","Inferior","Anterior","Proximal","Medial"],"e":"<b>Cranial</b> (towards the head) and caudal (towards the tail) <b>may be used instead of superior and inferior</b>.","w":["Equivalent to caudal.","Front of the body.","Relative to the origin/trunk.","Relative to the midline."],"k":"Cranial ≈ superior; caudal ≈ inferior.","rn":[["","Which terms may replace superior and inferior?","Which terms may replace superior and inferior?","Cranial and caudal"]]},{"id":3106,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"Name the vertical plane that divides the body into anterior and posterior parts.","e":"Coronal: vertical, separating anterior and posterior. Sagittal: vertical, separating right and left. Transverse: horizontal, separating superior and inferior.","k":"Coronal.","a":["coronal","coronal plane","frontal","frontal plane"],"rn":[["","Coronal plane","Coronal plane","Vertical; Separates anterior and posterior"]]},{"id":3107,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"Give one other name for the transverse plane.","e":"The transverse plane is also called the axial or horizontal plane; it separates superior and inferior.","k":"Transverse = axial = horizontal.","a":["axial","horizontal","axial plane","horizontal plane"],"rn":[["","What are 2 other names for the transverse plane?","What are 2 other names for the transverse plane?","Axial; Horizontal"],["","Transverse plane","Transverse plane","Also axial or horizontal; Separates superior and inferior"],["","Which plane separates superior from inferior?","Which plane separates superior from inferior?","Transverse (axial / horizontal)"]]},{"id":3108,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Name the position in which a patient lies face down.","e":"Prone = lying face down (e.g. spinal surgery). Supine = lying on the back.","k":"Prone.","a":["prone"],"rn":[["","What are the 2 lying positions on the front or back?","What are the 2 lying positions on the front or back?","Prone; Supine"],["","How do you remember pronation?","How do you remember pronation?","Hands lying prone (face down)"],["","What is the prone position used for?","What is the prone position used for?","Examining the back; Spinal surgery"]]},{"id":3109,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A gymnast points the toes so the sole faces the floor. Name the movement at the ankle.","e":"Plantarflexion: sole and toes point towards the floor. Dorsiflexion is the opposite.","k":"Plantarflexion.","a":["plantarflexion","plantar flexion","plantarflex"],"rn":[["","Plantarflexion","Plantarflexion","Sole and toes; Point towards the floor"]]},{"id":3110,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Name the movement in which the palm turns from facing forwards to facing backwards.","e":"Pronation: the palmar surface of the hand and forearm rotate to face backwards. Supination is the reverse.","k":"Pronation.","a":["pronation","pronate"],"rn":[["","Pronation","Pronation","Palm and forearm rotate; To face backwards"],["","In pronation, which way does the hand face?","In pronation, which way does the hand face?","Backwards"]]},{"id":3111,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"Which statement about the median plane is <b>false</b>?","o":["It separates anterior from posterior parts","It is a sagittal plane","It lies in the midline","It is also called the midsagittal plane","It splits the body into equal right and left halves"],"e":"The median (midsagittal) plane is a <b>sagittal</b> plane in the <b>midline</b>, dividing the body into equal right and left halves. Separating anterior from posterior is the <b>coronal</b> plane.","w":["True.","True.","True.","True."],"k":"Median plane = midline sagittal.","rn":[["","Coronal plane","Coronal plane","Vertical; Separates anterior and posterior"]]},{"id":3112,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A patient's ankle is moved so the dorsum of the foot moves upwards and then the sole is turned to face away from the midline. Which movements are these?","o":["Dorsiflexion, then eversion","Plantarflexion, then inversion","Dorsiflexion, then inversion","Plantarflexion, then eversion","Extension, then abduction"],"e":"Dorsum moving upwards = <b>dorsiflexion</b>. Sole facing away from the midline = <b>eversion</b>.","w":["Both reversed.","Sole away is eversion.","Dorsum up is dorsiflexion.","Use the specific foot terms."],"k":"Foot: dorsiflexion/plantarflexion, inversion/eversion.","rn":[["","Which movement turns the sole of the foot away from the midline?","Which movement turns the sole of the foot away from the midline?","Eversion"],["","Dorsiflexion","Dorsiflexion","Dorsum of foot and toes; Move upwards"],["","Eversion","Eversion","Foot turned outwards; Sole faces away from midline"]]},{"id":3113,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"What single term means “further from the trunk or origin”?","e":"Distal = further from the trunk or origin; proximal = closer to it.","k":"Distal.","a":["distal"],"rn":[["","Distal","Distal","Further from trunk or origin"]]},{"id":3114,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"Which named position would a surgeon most likely use for a right renal operation?","o":["Left lateral recumbent","Right lateral recumbent","Prone","Supine","Fowler's"],"e":"<b>Left lateral recumbent</b> is used for <b>right hip, right renal surgery</b> and DRE.","w":["For left hip/renal.","Back/spine.","Abdominal exam.","Shoulder/head and neck."],"k":"Left lateral recumbent → right renal surgery.","rn":[["","What is the right lateral recumbent position used for?","What is the right lateral recumbent position used for?","Left hip surgery; Left renal surgery"],["","What surgery uses the left lateral recumbent position?","What surgery uses the left lateral recumbent position?","Right hip surgery; Right renal surgery"],["","What are the 2 lying positions on the side?","What are the 2 lying positions on the side?","Right lateral recumbent; Left lateral recumbent"]]},{"id":3115,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"Which pairing of movement and definition is <b>incorrect</b>?","o":["Adduction – movement away from the midline","Flexion – decreases the angle at the joint","Supination – palmar surface faces forwards","Plantarflexion – sole and toes point towards the floor","Inversion – sole faces towards the midline"],"e":"<b>Adduction</b> is movement <b>towards</b> the midline; abduction is away from it.","w":["Correct.","Correct.","Correct.","Correct."],"k":"Adduction = towards midline.","rn":[["","Adduction","Adduction","Movement towards the midline"],["","Abduction","Abduction","Movement away from the midline"]]},{"id":3116,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"Is it correct to describe the knee as distal to the hip and proximal to the ankle?","o":["Both are correct","Both are wrong: proximal/distal apply only to the arm","Only “distal to the hip” is correct","Only “proximal to the ankle” is correct","Both are wrong: superior/inferior must be used"],"e":"<b>Proximal</b> = closer to the trunk/origin; <b>distal</b> = further from it. Along the lower limb, the knee is further from the trunk than the hip and closer than the ankle.","w":["They apply to limbs generally.","Both are correct.","Both are correct.","Proximal/distal are correct in limbs."],"k":"Apply proximal/distal along a limb.","rn":[["","Proximal and distal are relative to what?","Proximal and distal are relative to what?","A structure's origin"],["","Which 2 distal parts make up the lower limb?","Which 2 distal parts make up the lower limb?","Leg; Ankle and foot"]]},{"id":3117,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"Name the plane of a horizontal CT slice through the thorax.","e":"A horizontal slice is in the transverse (axial, horizontal) plane, separating superior from inferior.","k":"Transverse/axial.","a":["transverse","axial","horizontal","transverse plane","axial plane"],"rn":[["","Transverse plane","Transverse plane","Also axial or horizontal; Separates superior and inferior"],["","Which plane separates superior from inferior?","Which plane separates superior from inferior?","Transverse (axial / horizontal)"],["","What are 2 other names for the transverse plane?","What are 2 other names for the transverse plane?","Axial; Horizontal"]]},{"id":3118,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"Which pair of positions is linked to <b>surgery on the head and neck</b> and to <b>patients who have fainted</b>, respectively?","o":["Fowler's; Trendelenburg","Trendelenburg; Fowler's","Prone; supine","Supine; prone","Left lateral recumbent; right lateral recumbent"],"e":"<b>Fowler's</b>: shoulder, head and neck surgery. <b>Trendelenburg</b>: patients who have fainted, lower-limb surgery, gynaecological surgery.","w":["Reversed.","Prone is back/spine; supine is abdomen/lower limb.","Reversed.","Renal/hip/DRE."],"k":"Fowler's: head/neck/shoulder. Trendelenburg: fainting.","rn":[["","What is the Fowler's position used for?","What is the Fowler's position used for?","Shoulder surgery; Head and neck surgery"],["","Which 2 other named positions exist?","Which 2 other named positions exist?","Fowler's; Trendelenburg"],["","What is the Trendelenburg position used for?","What is the Trendelenburg position used for?","Patients who have fainted; Lower limb surgery; Gynaecological surgery (lithotomy)"]]},{"id":3119,"t":"What Is Anatomy","s":"Which statement about where gross and microscopic anatomy fit in medical teaching is correct?","o":["Gross: no magnification; histology: magnification","Gross needs magnification; histology is visible without it","Both gross anatomy and histology need magnification","Histology is the default meaning of “anatomy”","Gross anatomy needs an electron microscope"],"e":"<b>Gross anatomy</b> – visible <b>without</b> magnification; <b>microscopic anatomy (histology)</b> – visible <b>only with</b> magnification. “Anatomy” alone usually means gross anatomy.","w":["Reversed.","Gross does not.","Anatomy alone usually means gross.","Gross needs no magnification."],"k":"Gross vs microscopic.","rn":[["","Gross vs microscopic anatomy","Gross vs microscopic anatomy","Gross: seen without magnification; Microscopic: needs magnification"]]},{"id":3120,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"A skin lesion lies on the back of the left lower limb, just above the ankle. Which description is best?","o":["Posterior left leg, just proximal to the ankle","Anterior left leg, just proximal to the ankle","Posterior left leg, just distal to the ankle","Posterior left leg, just proximal to the knee","Posterior right leg, just proximal to the ankle"],"e":"Combine the <b>region</b> (left leg), the <b>term</b> for the back (<b>posterior</b>) and the position relative to a landmark (just <b>proximal</b> to the ankle, i.e. closer to the trunk).","w":["The back is posterior.","Above the ankle is proximal.","Just above the ankle.","It is the left leg."],"k":"Region + term + landmark = accurate description.","rn":[["","What is an example of combining regions, planes and terms?","What is an example of combining regions, planes and terms?","Transverse plane through left leg; Just proximal to the ankle"],["","Which plane would pass through the left leg just proximal to the ankle?","Which plane would pass through the left leg just proximal to the ankle?","Transverse plane"]]},{"id":3121,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A 34-year-old woman is brought to the emergency department after a fall. She is lying on a trolley with her arms by her sides and her palms resting on her thighs facing backwards. The junior doctor finds a cut on the side of the forearm nearest the little finger, close to the wrist. The registrar reminds him that all descriptions must use the standard reference position, whatever position the patient is actually in.<br><br>How should the cut be described?","o":["Medial and distal forearm, described with the forearm supinated","Lateral and proximal forearm, described with the forearm pronated","Medial and proximal forearm, described with the palm facing backwards","Lateral and distal forearm, described with the palms facing backwards as she lies","Posterior and proximal forearm, because the palm is facing backwards on the trolley"],"e":"Step 1: descriptions always refer to the anatomical position, in which the hands face forwards and the forearm is supinated (\"like holding a bowl of soup\"), with the thumbs out to the side. Step 2: in that position the little-finger side is nearer the midline (medial), and near the wrist means further from the trunk/origin (distal), so the cut is on the medial, distal forearm.","w":["In the anatomical position the thumb side is lateral, and near the wrist is distal.","Near the wrist is distal, not proximal, and her current posture is not the reference.","The little-finger side is medial when the forearm is supinated.","Her current (pronated) posture is not used; the anatomical position is."],"k":"Always describe in the anatomical position (forearm supinated)."},{"id":3122,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"A surgeon is planning operations for three patients. Patient 1 needs right kidney (renal) surgery. Patient 2 needs a digital rectal examination. Patient 3 needs surgery on the left hip. The theatre staff must position each patient on their side correctly.<br><br>Which positions are correct?","o":["1 and 2: left lateral recumbent; 3: right lateral recumbent","1 and 2: right lateral recumbent; 3: left lateral recumbent","1: prone; 2: supine; 3: Fowler's position with the head of the table raised up","All three: left lateral recumbent with the knees flexed","1: Trendelenburg; 2: left lateral recumbent; 3: supine"],"e":"Step 1: the patient lies on the side opposite the operated hip or kidney, so the operated side faces up. Step 2: left lateral recumbent is used for right hip and right renal surgery and for digital rectal examination (with knees flexed), while right lateral recumbent is used for left hip and left renal surgery.","w":["The sides are reversed: right renal surgery uses left lateral recumbent.","Prone is for the back and Fowler's is for shoulder, head and neck surgery.","Left hip surgery needs right lateral recumbent.","Trendelenburg is for fainting, lower-limb and gynaecological surgery."],"k":"Lie on the side opposite the operated hip/kidney; DRE = left lateral.","rn":[["","What is the right lateral recumbent position used for?","What is the right lateral recumbent position used for?","Left hip surgery; Left renal surgery"],["","What surgery uses the left lateral recumbent position?","What surgery uses the left lateral recumbent position?","Right hip surgery; Right renal surgery"],["","What are the 2 lying positions on the side?","What are the 2 lying positions on the side?","Right lateral recumbent; Left lateral recumbent"]]},{"id":3123,"t":"Anatomical Position","s":"On a busy surgical day, a theatre nurse prepares four patients. One has fainted in the waiting area. One is having shoulder surgery. One is having spinal surgery. One is having gynaecological surgery with the legs supported up in stirrups.<br><br>Which pairing of patient and position is correct?","o":["Shoulder surgery: Fowler's; spinal surgery: prone","Fainted patient: Fowler's; gynaecological surgery: prone","Shoulder surgery: Trendelenburg; spinal surgery: supine","Spinal surgery: supine; fainted patient: left lateral recumbent","Gynaecological surgery: Fowler's with the legs held in the lithotomy position"],"e":"Step 1: Fowler's position is used for shoulder surgery and head and neck surgery, and prone (face down) is used to examine the back and for spinal surgery. Step 2: Trendelenburg is used for the fainted patient and for gynaecological surgery (with the lower limbs in a lithotomy position), so only the first pairing is fully correct.","w":["Fainting uses Trendelenburg, and gynaecological surgery is not prone.","Shoulder surgery uses Fowler's, and spinal surgery is prone.","Spinal surgery is prone, and fainting uses Trendelenburg.","Gynaecological surgery uses Trendelenburg with lithotomy."],"k":"Fowler's: shoulder/head/neck; prone: spine; Trendelenburg: faint, gynae.","rn":[["","What is the Fowler's position used for?","What is the Fowler's position used for?","Shoulder surgery; Head and neck surgery"],["","What is the prone position used for?","What is the prone position used for?","Examining the back; Spinal surgery"]]},{"id":3124,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"A radiologist reviews a scan of a patient's abdomen. In one image, she can see structures at the front and back of the body and on the right and left, but not the parts above and below the slice. In a second image she can see structures from the head end to the feet end and from right to left, but not the front and back at once.<br><br>In which planes were the two images taken?","o":["Image 1: transverse (axial); image 2: coronal","Image 1: coronal; image 2: sagittal","Image 1: median sagittal; image 2: transverse","Image 1: sagittal; image 2: transverse","Image 1: coronal; image 2: axial, because it separates the head end from the feet end"],"e":"Step 1: the transverse (axial, horizontal) plane separates superior from inferior parts, so its cut surface shows anterior–posterior and left–right together. Step 2: the coronal plane is vertical and separates anterior from posterior, so its cut surface shows superior–inferior and right–left.","w":["A coronal slice would show up–down, not front–back; a sagittal slice separates right and left.","A median sagittal slice splits right and left halves.","A sagittal slice cannot show both right and left together.","The second image shows up–down, so it is coronal, not axial."],"k":"Axial shows front–back + left–right; coronal shows up–down + left–right.","rn":[["","Transverse plane","Transverse plane","Also axial or horizontal; Separates superior and inferior"],["","Which plane separates superior from inferior?","Which plane separates superior from inferior?","Transverse (axial / horizontal)"],["","What are the 4 anatomical planes?","What are the 4 anatomical planes?","Coronal; Sagittal; Midsagittal; Transverse"]]},{"id":3125,"t":"Anatomical Planes","s":"A surgeon describes an incision in the operation notes as being in \"a sagittal plane through the thorax, medial to the nipple\". A medical student reading the notes assumes that the incision must run exactly down the middle of the chest. The consultant says the student has misunderstood.<br><br>Which statement best explains the consultant's view?","o":["Any sagittal plane separates right and left; only the median plane is in the midline","A sagittal plane separates anterior from posterior, so it cannot be in the midline of the thorax","Medial means further from the midline, so the incision is lateral to the nipple","A sagittal plane is horizontal and separates superior and inferior","The median plane is the coronal plane through the midline"],"e":"Step 1: a sagittal plane is vertical and separates right and left parts, and only the median (midsagittal) plane passes through the midline giving equal halves. Step 2: \"medial to the nipple\" only means closer to the midline than the nipple, so the incision lies in a sagittal plane between the nipple and the midline, not necessarily in the median plane.","w":["Separating anterior from posterior is the coronal plane.","Medial means closer to the midline.","Horizontal planes are transverse/axial.","The median plane is a sagittal plane."],"k":"Sagittal = right/left; median = the midline sagittal plane only.","rn":[["","Sagittal plane","Sagittal plane","Vertical; Separates right and left"],["","Midsagittal (median) plane","Midsagittal (median) plane","Down the midline; Equal right and left halves"]]},{"id":3126,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"A patient has two moles on the same lower limb. Mole P is on the thigh and mole Q is just above the ankle. Mole P is also nearer the skin surface than a deep lump found beneath it. The clinician must record the relationship between the two moles and between mole P and the lump.<br><br>Which description is correct?","o":["P is proximal to Q; P is superficial to the lump","P is distal to Q; P is superficial to the lump","P is proximal to Q; P is deep to the lump","P is distal to Q; the lump is superficial to P","P is lateral to Q and medial to the lump"],"e":"Step 1: proximal/distal are relative to a structure's origin or the trunk, so the thigh mole (closer to the trunk) is proximal to the ankle mole. Step 2: superficial/deep are relative to the body surface, so mole P, closer to the surface, is superficial to the deeper lump.","w":["The thigh is closer to the trunk than the ankle, so P is proximal.","P is nearer the surface, so it is superficial, not deep.","Both relationships are reversed.","Medial/lateral relate to the median plane, not to position along a limb."],"k":"Proximal/distal → origin/trunk; superficial/deep → surface."},{"id":3127,"t":"Anatomical Terms","s":"A 70-year-old man has a stroke affecting the left side of his brain. He develops weakness of his right arm and right leg. His left limbs are normal. The neurologist records the weakness in relation to the side of the brain lesion, using the paired terms of location.<br><br>Which term describes the weakness in relation to the lesion?","o":["Contralateral, because it is on the opposite side","Ipsilateral, because it is on the same side","Lateral, because the limbs are away from the midline","Distal, because the limbs are further from the brain","Caudal, because the limbs are towards the tail end of the body"],"e":"Step 1: the paired terms ipsilateral and contralateral compare the side of one structure with another. Step 2: ipsilateral means the same side and contralateral means the opposite side, so right-sided weakness from a left-sided lesion is contralateral.","w":["Ipsilateral means same side, but the weakness is on the opposite side.","Lateral compares distance from the median plane, not side to side.","Distal describes distance along a structure from its origin.","Caudal means towards the tail and does not describe sides."],"k":"Ipsilateral = same side; contralateral = opposite side.","rn":[["","Contralateral","Contralateral","opposite side"],["","Which pairs of terms describe side?","Which pairs of terms describe side?","Right or left; Ipsilateral or contralateral"]]},{"id":3128,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A physiotherapist assesses a 50-year-old man's arm after an injury. She asks him to turn his hand so that the palm faces backwards, then to bend his elbow, and then to move his whole arm out to the side away from his body. Finally he moves the arm so that the hand traces a circle while the shoulder stays fixed.<br><br>Which sequence of movements has he performed?","o":["Pronation, flexion, abduction, circumduction","Supination, extension, adduction, rotation","Pronation, extension, adduction, circumduction","Supination, flexion, abduction, lateral rotation","Pronation, flexion, adduction, medial rotation of the whole limb"],"e":"Step 1: turning the forearm so the palm faces backwards is pronation, and bending the elbow decreases the joint angle, which is flexion. Step 2: moving away from the midline is abduction, and moving so that the distal end describes a circle while the proximal end is fixed is circumduction.","w":["Supination turns the palm forwards, and bending is flexion.","Bending the elbow is flexion, and moving away from the body is abduction.","Palm backwards is pronation, and a circle is circumduction.","Moving away from the midline is abduction, and the last movement is circumduction."],"k":"Pronation (palm back), flexion (angle ↓), abduction (away), circumduction."},{"id":3129,"t":"Terms of Movement","s":"A 25-year-old footballer twists his ankle. On assessment, he can lift the top of his foot and toes upwards and can point his toes towards the floor. However, he has pain when he turns his foot inward so that the sole faces the midline. Turning the sole outwards away from the midline is painless.<br><br>Which movement is painful, and which movements are normal?","o":["Inversion is painful; dorsiflexion, plantarflexion and eversion are normal","Eversion is painful; dorsiflexion and plantarflexion are normal","Dorsiflexion is painful; inversion and eversion are normal","Plantarflexion is painful; inversion is normal and eversion is painful","Inversion is painful; abduction, adduction and medial rotation of the foot are all normal"],"e":"Step 1: lifting the dorsum of the foot upwards is dorsiflexion and pointing the sole and toes to the floor is plantarflexion, both of which are normal. Step 2: turning the foot inward so the sole faces the midline is inversion (painful), while turning it outward so the sole faces away is eversion (painless).","w":["Turning the sole away from the midline (eversion) is painless.","Lifting the foot (dorsiflexion) is normal here.","Pointing the toes (plantarflexion) is normal and eversion is painless.","The named foot movements are dorsiflexion, plantarflexion, inversion and eversion."],"k":"Inversion: sole to midline; eversion: away; dorsi/plantarflexion: up/down."},{"id":3130,"t":"What Is Anatomy","s":"A medical student studies a liver in two ways. First she looks at the whole organ in the dissecting room and describes its surfaces and relations without any magnification. Then she examines a thin stained section of the same liver under a microscope to see its cells. Her tutor asks which branch of anatomy each activity belongs to.<br><br>Which answer is correct?","o":["The first is gross anatomy; the second is microscopic anatomy (histology)","The first is histology; the second is gross anatomy","Both are gross anatomy, as both study the liver","The first is microscopic anatomy; the second is living anatomy","Both are histology, as both use stained sections"],"e":"Step 1: gross anatomy covers structures visible without magnification (and \"anatomy\" alone usually means gross anatomy). Step 2: microscopic anatomy (histology) covers structures visible only with magnification, such as cells in a stained section.","w":["The two are reversed.","Viewing cells needs magnification, so the second is microscopic.","Viewing the whole organ without magnification is gross anatomy.","Only the second used magnification."],"k":"Gross = no magnification; histology = with magnification.","rn":[["","Gross vs microscopic anatomy","Gross vs microscopic anatomy","Gross: seen without magnification; Microscopic: needs magnification"],["","Microscopic anatomy is also called?","Microscopic anatomy is also called?","Histology"]]}]);
